[38;5;231m⏺[39m Classification: [1mbounded[0m — [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m already exists and this adds one function to it, so I'll present a short design
  in chat rather than write a spec.

  Current state: a 2-file CommonJS project ([38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m with [38;5;153mgreet[39m, [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m), no test runner and no dependencies.

  One thing matters before I write anything — how literally to take the four rules:

  - [1mLiteral[0m: implement exactly the four checks as stated. [38;5;153m"a@.com"[39m, [38;5;153m"a@com."[39m, and [38;5;153m"a@b@c.com"[39m all return [38;5;153mtrue[39m, because
    each one does contain an [38;5;153m@[39m, has a character before it, and has a dot after it. Cheap to write, cheap to tighten
    later, but it will pass through addresses most people would call malformed.
  - [1mLiteral + guards[0m: same four rules, plus require exactly one [38;5;153m@[39m and require the dot to have at least one character on
    each side within the domain. Rejects the three cases above. Still no regex zoo, still no RFC 5322 ambition — just
    closes the holes the literal reading leaves open.
  - [1mFull RFC-ish validation[0m: quoted local parts, IP-literal domains, length limits. Much more surface area, and
    genuinely hard to get right; I'd recommend against it for this.

  This is the choice that's expensive to change later, because it's the observable behavior other code will depend on.
  My recommendation is [1mliteral + guards[0m: it matches everything you asked for, and the extra two conditions are a few
  lines that prevent obviously-bad input from being accepted.
